<DocsLayout lang="en" title="Security" description="Protect confidential data, agent access and privileged sources without granting unnecessary permissions.">
  <h2>Core rules</h2>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Never commit <code>.env</code> files, keys or host configurations.</li>
    <li>Use at least twelve characters for the dashboard password and a unique strong key for each agent.</li>
    <li>Use HTTPS and WebSocket over TLS for all network access.</li>
    <li>Keep dashboard state and configuration files readable only by the service user.</li>
    <li>Do not expose agent ports unnecessarily. Push agents require no inbound port.</li>
    <li>Back up the state directory with encryption and test restoration.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Encrypted agent communication</h2>
  <p>Push agents authenticate with their agent name and <code>X-Agent-Key</code>. They verify the dashboard TLS certificate. <code>wss://</code> is mandatory for remote connections; <code>ws://</code> is accepted only for loopback connections.</p>
  <p>The proxy between dashboard and agent must support WebSocket upgrades. Restrict the internal hop between proxy and dashboard as well, because keys and data are forwarded there.</p>

  <h2>Rotate keys</h2>
  <ol><li>Generate a new random key, for example with <code>openssl rand -base64 32</code>.</li><li>Add it to the appropriate agent configuration in the dashboard.</li><li>Restart the dashboard in a controlled manner.</li><li>Update the agent and restart its service.</li><li>Verify that no processes or logs still contain the old key.</li></ol>

  <h2>Agent permissions</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Access</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody><tr><td>Read-only /proc</td><td>CPU, RAM and kernel metrics</td><td>On</td></tr><tr><td>Read-only /</td><td>Host filesystem usage</td><td>Off</td></tr><tr><td>Docker socket</td><td>Container status, metrics and events</td><td>Off, root-equivalent</td></tr><tr><td>Journal</td><td>SSH and Fail2Ban events</td><td>Off</td></tr><tr><td>NET_ADMIN and UFW</td><td>Firewall status</td><td>Off</td></tr><tr><td>Fail2Ban socket</td><td>Fail2Ban status and events</td><td>Off</td></tr></tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Legacy HTTP agents</h2>
  <p>Older pull agents remain compatible when <code>NODESENTINEL_HUB_URL</code> is not set. Every request authenticates with <code>X-Agent-Key</code>. Outside loopback, TLS with certificate verification is mandatory.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="en" code={`openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -nodes -days 825 \\
  -keyout agent.key -out agent.crt \\
  -subj "/CN=homelab1.lan" \\
  -addext "subjectAltName=DNS:homelab1.lan,IP:192.168.1.20"
chmod 600 agent.key`} />
  <p>Distribute only <code>agent.crt</code> to the dashboard. Certificate verification cannot be disabled.</p>
  <div class="callout warning"><strong>Trust boundaries</strong><p>The Docker socket, write access to host paths, NET_ADMIN, and Fail2Ban or UFW helpers significantly change the security boundary. Run only trusted images and evaluate each source individually.</p></div>
</DocsLayout>
